1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,202
The epidural, the anaesthesia we all associate

2
00:00:03,014 --> 00:00:05,771
with childbirth, was first described by a

3
00:00:05,583 --> 00:00:06,757
Spanish surgeon.

4
00:00:06,886 --> 00:00:08,955
And almost nobody knows his name.

5
00:00:09,382 --> 00:00:12,403
Fidel Pagés, a military surgeon born in

6
00:00:12,215 --> 00:00:12,965
Huesca.

7
00:00:13,093 --> 00:00:16,115
In 1921 he publishes his technique in a

8
00:00:15,926 --> 00:00:17,043
Spanish journal:

9
00:00:17,256 --> 00:00:20,782
anatomy, method, cases and complications.

10
00:00:21,202 --> 00:00:23,719
He called it metameric anaesthesia, because it

11
00:00:23,530 --> 00:00:25,083
numbs the body in bands.

12
00:00:25,212 --> 00:00:26,937
It's like switching off the lights on

13
00:00:26,748 --> 00:00:28,852
a single floor of a building, not

14
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:30,228
on every floor below.

15
00:00:30,646 --> 00:00:33,025
Two years later, Pagés dies in a

16
00:00:32,836 --> 00:00:33,919
road accident.

17
00:00:34,048 --> 00:00:35,372
He was 37.

18
00:00:35,794 --> 00:00:39,755
In the 1930s, the Italian Dogliotti popularizes

19
00:00:39,567 --> 00:00:41,659
his own version without crediting him.

20
00:00:41,788 --> 00:00:45,795
Until an Argentinian surgeon, Alberto Gutiérrez, insists…

21
00:00:45,870 --> 00:00:49,006
and in 1939 Dogliotti acknowledges him.

22
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:51,633
Today the medical literature is clear:

23
00:00:51,823 --> 00:00:54,443
the epidural was first described by Fidel

24
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:56,163
Pagés in 1921.

25
00:00:56,578 --> 00:00:57,994
Share it with someone who has had

26
00:00:57,805 --> 00:00:58,292
one.

